course: dictionary
lesson: 9146
title: Words for laughing
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words for laughing are warm and friendly. Others are sneaky. To snigger is to laugh quietly in a rude or unkind way, often at someone else's mistake. «Jake says "Sorry, innit" to Sam with a big smirk while his mates snigger.» The smirk and the quiet laughing show that the boys are being unkind, not joking with Sam.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. Sniggering usually happens behind someone's back, so the person being laughed at often cannot hear it. A snigger is also the small, half-hidden laugh itself. Ask: what would the people in the story be doing? If the answer is laughing at someone, you have found the meaning.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. «Jake says "Sorry, innit" to Sam with a big smirk while his mates laugh quietly in an unkind way» still makes sense, so you have the right idea. In American English the usual word is snicker, which means the same thing.
fact: To snigger means to laugh quietly in a rude or unkind way.
fact: A snigger is the small, half-hidden laugh itself: «a snigger went round the class».
fact: «Snigger» can be a verb or a noun, so you can snigger, or you can give a snigger.
fact: Snigger at someone means to laugh unkindly at them, often at their mistake.
fact: In American English the usual word is «snicker», which means the same.
exercise: «Jake says "Sorry, innit" to Sam with a big smirk while his mates snigger.» What does snigger mean here? | To clap happily for a friend | *To laugh quietly in a rude or unkind way | To cry loudly in a sad way | To shout a warning
exercise: «Sam gave a wrong answer, and a snigger went round the class.» What is a snigger? | A loud song sung by the whole class | A kind hug from a friend | *A small, half-hidden laugh | A sharp pencil
exercise: «Sam was too shy to answer, so the class began to snigger.» Find and copy one word that means to laugh quietly and unkindly. | answer | class | *snigger | shy
test: «Maya tripped on the stairs, and the boys began to snigger at her.» What does snigger at mean here? | Say sorry to someone | Hide from someone | Clap to cheer for someone | *Laugh unkindly at someone
test: Ben asks, «Is snicker the same as snigger?» What is the answer? | *Yes, in American English snicker means the same as snigger | No, snicker means a loud shout | Yes, but snicker means to cry | No, snicker is a type of sweet
exam: Lily says, «Some teachers stop children from sniggering when someone gets an answer wrong.» Why do teachers stop sniggering? | Because sniggering is always kind and helpful | *Because sniggering can be unkind to the person who got it wrong | Because sniggering makes the room too quiet to hear | Because they want the class to laugh more
